.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt OPENEIGRPD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm openeigrpd
.Nd native Linux EIGRP routing daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl f Ar config
.Op Fl s Ar socket
.Op Fl t
.Op Fl V
.Op Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs the portable OpenEIGRP protocol engine on Linux using raw IP protocol
88 sockets, interface and route Netlink services, and a local management
socket.
The daemon runs in the foreground; it does not fork into the background.
The portable core owns DUAL, topology, metric calculation, neighbor state,
packetization, and reliable transport.
.Pp
The packaged
.Xr systemd 1
service runs as the
.Sy openeigrp
system user with
.Dv CAP_NET_RAW
and
.Dv CAP_NET_ADMIN .
Installation does not automatically start or enable routing.
The service starts only after an administrator enables it, or starts it
explicitly.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f Ar config
Read the named-mode configuration from the absolute pathname
.Ar config .
The default is
.Pa /etc/openeigrpd.conf .
.It Fl s Ar socket
Bind the Unix-domain management socket at the absolute pathname
.Ar socket .
The default is
.Pa /run/openeigrpd/control.sock .
The parent directory must exist and must be a real directory owned by root
or the daemon user, without group/world write permissions.
.It Fl t
Parse and validate the configuration syntax, then exit without
initializing routing, packet sockets, or kernel routes.
This does not prove that a semantic operation will succeed at runtime.
.It Fl V
Print the portable OpenEIGRP release identifier and exit.
.It Fl h
Display usage and exit.
.El
.Sh STARTUP AND SHUTDOWN
At startup,
.Nm
parses the entire configuration file, initializes host and portable services,
then applies the configuration through portable semantic APIs.
An invalid or unsupported semantic operation aborts startup with cleanup
and a nonzero exit status.
Only after successful configuration does it publish the management socket.
.Pp
During operation the daemon processes interface and route Netlink events
and periodically reconciles the desired kernel RIB with the host RIB.
It logs startup, configuration failures, socket failures, rejected reloads,
and host processing errors to standard error, captured by the systemd
journal when started by the packaged service.
.Pp
Shutdown first stops management access, then terminates protocol workers,
removes EIGRP-owned routes and releases host and configuration resources.
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width SIGTERM
.It Dv SIGTERM
Request orderly shutdown.
.It Dv SIGINT
Request orderly shutdown.
.It Dv SIGHUP
Log that reload is unsupported; retain the active configuration unchanged.
Validate the new file and restart the service instead.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/openeigrpd.conf
Root-controlled configuration file, normally mode 0600.
The Debian package installs it as a preserved conffile.
.It Pa /run/openeigrpd/control.sock
Management socket, mode 0660, normally accessible to the daemon and the
.Sy openeigrpctl
group.
.It Pa /usr/lib/systemd/system/openeigrpd.service
Packaged service unit.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Validate an IPv4/IPv6 configuration without starting routing:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
openeigrpd -t -f /etc/openeigrpd.conf
.Ed
.Pp
Start and inspect the packaged service:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo systemctl enable --now openeigrpd
sudo systemctl status openeigrpd
sudo oectl show protocols
.Ed
.Pp
Use a dedicated socket for a foreground development run:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo openeigrpd -f /etc/openeigrpd.conf -s /run/openeigrpd/control.sock
.Ed
.Sh SECURITY
Raw EIGRP packets and RIB changes need appropriate network privileges.
Do not grant those privileges to the read-only control client.
The socket's filesystem permissions grant observational access; the daemon
additionally verifies peer credentials before permitting
.Cm clear events .
Only root or the daemon startup UID may issue that mutating command.
Do not use a group-writable socket directory or place secrets in a
world-readable configuration file.
.Sh LIMITATIONS
Only default-VRF unicast address families are accepted by the current
native configuration parser.
Live configuration reload, EIGRP Stub runtime, and unsupported
policy/authentication modes are not implemented.
Refer to
.Xr openeigrpd.conf 5
for the accepted configuration subset.
A successful syntax check does not guarantee interface, socket,
permissions or runtime semantic readiness.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr openeigrpd.conf 5 ,
.Xr oectl 8 ,
.Xr systemctl 1
